{"kind":"assert","text":"Evidence for the solo-adoption documentation work in request b7087c81, gathered after filing it. Two things, both of which change what that work should do. First, the world moved: main advanced to 3c6d676c, where Hugh added four lines to README.md stating that gitseq is a simple layer over git and a strong platform for multi-agent workflows, usable to accelerate development, strengthen review cycles, or build traceability into existing processes. That partly meets the request's first condition, and it makes the request's own evidence outdated where it says README leads with document currency and mentions agents only incidentally at line 76. The remaining scope is unaffected: the job is still not stated in the reader's words, docs/README.md is untouched, there is no two-route structure, and the operating prompt is still absent. Second, the safety envelope of gs init, verified by reading cmd/gs/main.go, internal/app/app.go, internal/kernel/kernel.go and internal/gitstore/gitstore.go rather than by inference, because a first adopter's real question is whether this can hurt the repository they already work in. It is safe but not idempotent: Init stats config.json under the git directory and returns workroom already initialized, so a second run errors rather than succeeding quietly, which a scripted example must expect. It creates only the gitseq directory inside the git directory, holding config.json, one generated actor key and a generated sequencer keypair; one parentless commit over an empty tree; and one new ref refs/seq/genesis. It touches no branch, not HEAD, not the index, and no working-tree file: the empty tree comes from mktree rather than write-tree and the commit from commit-tree, so a dirty worktree and uncommitted changes are irrelevant to it, and nothing appears in git status. The operator name is required from the flag or GITSEQ_ACTOR and it refuses to seed an unnamed operator. refs/seq is not in a default push refspec, so the workroom stays local until pushed deliberately, which is what makes the solo route work and is also the thing a reader will misunderstand. Complete removal is rm of the gitseq directory plus git update-ref -d of the refs/seq entry. The documentation page should state these, because the question was asked by the project's own author before running it."}